no_prelude

# `argv_get` is the ONE capability this design deliberately removes
# (docs/no-prelude-core-v0.md §8), and it is pinned here so the removal cannot be
# undone by accident.
#
# It is the odd one out: unlike `vector_*` and friends it DOES work in a
# `no_prelude` file today — it is one of the nine names the lowerer already
# treats as an unconditional intrinsic, so it has both a scheme (via the leak)
# and a declare, and it compiles, links and runs. It leaves anyway, because its
# signature is `Int -> Maybe String !{IO}` and `Maybe` is a prelude-declared type.
# Admitting it would mean admitting `Maybe` to the floor, and a floor carrying a
# prelude ADT lands a `Maybe` in the namespace of exactly the files most likely to
# declare their own — which re-triggers both open qualification-uniformity bugs
# (`__cm_<Class>_<method>` mangling and do-notation's bare-name monad lookup).
#
# That is not a trade worth making for one name: reading argv from a file that
# has opted out of the standard library is a thin use case, and nothing in the
# repo does it.

fn main() -> Int !{IO} =
  match argv_get(0) with
  | Just _ -> 1
  | Nothing -> 0
